From ebd56dcd9228f67682a51ff996c4950db04ea52e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 14:55:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Serve the right macOS build, and widen the Linux options
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

BlinkBlink releases changed shape: macOS now ships an Intel and an Apple
Silicon DMG, and Linux ships AppImage, deb and rpm alongside the snap.

The download page picked assets with `name.endsWith('.dmg')`, which
returns whichever the GitHub API happens to list first — so about half of
macOS visitors would have been handed a build for the wrong processor,
and an arm64 build does not launch on an Intel Mac at all. macOS
downloads now detect the architecture (User-Agent Client Hints where it
exists, the WebGL renderer string on Safari, Apple Silicon as the
default) and pick a matching asset, falling back to an unsuffixed one for
releases published before architectures were in the filenames. Detection
is a heuristic, so the page says which build it served and links to the
releases page for the other.

Windows deliberately needs none of this: it ships x64 only, because
electron-updater's latest.yml has no per-architecture entry and
publishing both would hand ARM64 installers to x64 machines on
auto-update. Windows on ARM emulates x64.

The Linux button now downloads the AppImage, with .deb, .rpm and the Snap
Store offered underneath — Fedora users cannot use a snap without setting
one up first.

Two smaller fixes:

- `onMounted` read `releases[0]` from the unsorted, unfiltered API
  response, so a prerelease could be advertised as the current version
  while the buttons served something else. Both paths now share one
  helper that excludes drafts and prereleases and sorts by version rather
  than publication date, so re-publishing an old release cannot advertise
  it as current.
- The install guide told people to run `xattr -c`, which clears every
  extended attribute when only the quarantine flag is in the way. It now
  uses `xattr -d com.apple.quarantine`, and leads with the System
  Settings route that needs no Terminal at all.

Also adds the Chinese string for `hero.feedbackButton`, which was used in
Home.vue but missing from zh.json.

Verified with `npm run type-check` and `npm run build`, and by rendering
the built page in both locales. `npm run lint` crashes on this repo
before and after this change — an eslint 9.15 / typescript-eslint version
mismatch, unrelated and left alone.

